3138
03:34:11,888 --> 03:34:13,028
Okay, so what am I talking about?

3139
03:34:15,048 --> 03:34:18,728
Transitivity, nice mathematical property for certain things

3140
03:34:18,828 --> 03:34:20,928
like greater than and less than is transitive,

3141
03:34:22,228 --> 03:34:25,768
inequality isn't transitive, and then there's things like, I think,

3142
03:34:27,048 --> 03:34:30,868
the relationship that you get from rock, paper, scissors is a anti-transitive

3143
03:34:30,948 --> 03:34:33,888
relationship. But anyway, as it relates to name resolution,

3144
03:34:35,148 --> 03:34:38,308
if you have some taxonomy and you have a name,

3145
03:34:39,648 --> 03:34:40,988
so version one of your taxonomy,

3146
03:34:42,388 --> 03:34:46,288
and you have a name x, if that resolves to some name y in

3147
03:34:46,348 --> 03:34:47,008
your taxonomy,

3148
03:34:48,748 --> 03:34:51,988
and then you update your understanding and you update your taxonomy

3149
03:34:52,848 --> 03:34:54,108
in the future to version two,

3150
03:34:55,728 --> 03:34:57,368
and that name y then resolves to some name z.

3151
03:34:59,848 --> 03:35:01,748
This process is transitive if,

3152
03:35:03,048 --> 03:35:03,228
in

3153
03:35:04,648 --> 03:35:08,508
version two, the original name x also resolves

3154
03:35:08,568 --> 03:35:09,048
to z.

3155
03:35:11,008 --> 03:35:12,228
Now you can imagine that this

3156
03:35:13,448 --> 03:35:16,308
breaks for things like heterotypic synonyms.

3157
03:35:16,528 --> 03:35:20,368
So if in version one x is a heterotypic synonym

3158
03:35:20,448 --> 03:35:20,868
of y,

3159
03:35:22,848 --> 03:35:26,588
and then at some later point in time it's considered that those two type specimens

3160
03:35:26,748 --> 03:35:26,888
are

3161
03:35:28,088 --> 03:35:32,068
actually different taxa, then x might not be resolving to

3162
03:35:33,008 --> 03:35:33,088
z

3163
03:35:34,388 --> 03:35:35,228
in version two.

3164
03:35:37,088 --> 03:35:40,648
So this is important to consider for building data sets, because

3165
03:35:41,588 --> 03:35:44,908
at the time of version one of this taxonomy, if you

3166
03:35:46,488 --> 03:35:47,748
build some data set, you find

3167
03:35:49,588 --> 03:35:53,228
the name x and you resolve it to y, then when someone comes along in the future

3168
03:35:54,608 --> 03:35:55,928
with an updated taxonomy,

3169
03:35:58,008 --> 03:36:01,288
if they're then resolving y to z, that might differ from

3170
03:36:02,028 --> 03:36:05,028
our current understanding of how x should be resolved.

3171
03:36:07,188 --> 03:36:09,228
So yeah, we were interested in

3172
03:36:11,788 --> 03:36:15,288
how many names this fails for and if there's any patterns in the

3173
03:36:16,088 --> 03:36:17,988
places and ways that this fails.

