3591
05:30:42,266 --> 05:30:44,526
Hello, everyone. My name is Sara Araújo.

3592
05:30:44,986 --> 05:30:48,866
I'm a marine biologist working at the University of Aveiro

3593
05:30:48,906 --> 05:30:52,726
in Portugal, and I'll be presenting the Aurora Shiny app, a

3594
05:30:52,846 --> 05:30:56,746
tool developed to help standardizing messy biodiversity data sets according

3595
05:30:56,826 --> 05:30:57,726
to the Darwin Core.

3596
05:30:59,586 --> 05:31:03,386
To start with a small introduction, biodiversity data are often stored

3597
05:31:03,486 --> 05:31:06,866
in diverse structures, which makes standardization and

3598
05:31:06,946 --> 05:31:09,846
publication through public repositories challenging.

3599
05:31:11,566 --> 05:31:14,866
These repositories, such as GBIF and OBIS, require data to be

3600
05:31:14,906 --> 05:31:17,026
compliant with the Darwin Core standard.

3601
05:31:17,926 --> 05:31:21,666
But the standardization process can sometimes mean

3602
05:31:21,746 --> 05:31:25,666
overcoming complex event structures, taxonomic ambiguities, and

3603
05:31:26,325 --> 05:31:29,826
geospatial inaccuracies, which can make this whole process

3604
05:31:30,426 --> 05:31:32,166
effort, time, and resource-demanding.

3605
05:31:33,626 --> 05:31:37,106
Many researchers indicate this step as one of the main

3606
05:31:37,166 --> 05:31:39,226
barriers to publishing their data.

3607
05:31:40,066 --> 05:31:44,046
And it was with this in mind that we developed an online application

3608
05:31:44,086 --> 05:31:45,026
based on R Shiny

3609
05:31:45,926 --> 05:31:49,526
that offers a comprehensive and user-friendly workflow for

3610
05:31:49,586 --> 05:31:53,006
converting unstructured biodiversity data into

3611
05:31:53,746 --> 05:31:54,906
standardized Darwin Core

3612
05:31:55,766 --> 05:31:57,306
tables ready for publication.

3613
05:31:58,926 --> 05:32:01,686
Through this QR code, you can access

3614
05:32:03,046 --> 05:32:06,726
the tool's website. The Aurora Shiny app requires only an

3615
05:32:06,766 --> 05:32:10,206
internet connection to work, and the workflow is

3616
05:32:10,226 --> 05:32:14,086
structured around eight key steps, which I will be going through

3617
05:32:14,166 --> 05:32:15,386
in the next slides.

3618
05:32:16,726 --> 05:32:20,526
And to start with the first one, the Aurora Shiny app allows users to

3619
05:32:20,566 --> 05:32:23,326
upload their data as a CSV file.

3620
05:32:24,186 --> 05:32:27,886
And their tables can then be transformed to a tidy

3621
05:32:27,946 --> 05:32:30,906
format if necessary, so one

3622
05:32:33,166 --> 05:32:34,286
occurrence per record.

3623
05:32:36,226 --> 05:32:40,146
The tables can be uploaded already in a tidy format or transformed

3624
05:32:40,186 --> 05:32:41,546
through the application.

3625
05:32:43,106 --> 05:32:47,046
The data set can then be inspected before completing the ingestion, just

3626
05:32:47,146 --> 05:32:51,126
to make sure that the data was read correctly.

3627
05:32:54,106 --> 05:32:57,706
Then user-defined headers can be mapped to the

3628
05:32:57,766 --> 05:32:58,926
suitable Darwin Core term,

3629
05:32:59,806 --> 05:33:03,626
and this can be done either following automatic suggestions

3630
05:33:03,726 --> 05:33:04,506
or manually.

3631
05:33:05,426 --> 05:33:09,166
Even following automatic suggestions, users should always ensure

3632
05:33:09,226 --> 05:33:11,746
that the right term was

3633
05:33:12,706 --> 05:33:14,586
associated with their header.

3634
05:33:17,286 --> 05:33:21,246
The app also allows users to create key fields within the application, including

3635
05:33:21,346 --> 05:33:24,206
ID fields such as Occurrence ID or Events ID,

3636
05:33:25,446 --> 05:33:27,586
remarks fields, and dependency fields.

3637
05:33:28,266 --> 05:33:32,226
These dependency fields include, for instance, the

3638
05:33:32,266 --> 05:33:36,146
creation of sample size units if sample size value is

3639
05:33:36,206 --> 05:33:38,346
already present in the data set.

3640
05:33:40,686 --> 05:33:44,586
The workflow also includes the validation of required terms, and the set

3641
05:33:44,666 --> 05:33:46,826
of these required terms can be adjusted

3642
05:33:47,906 --> 05:33:50,286
by selecting the target submission repository.

3643
05:33:50,406 --> 05:33:54,306
So currently, users can choose between OBIS, GBIF, and

3644
05:33:54,386 --> 05:33:57,866
EMODnet Biology as their target submission repository.

3645
05:34:01,526 --> 05:34:05,466
The tool also allows the transformation of temporal information

3646
05:34:05,526 --> 05:34:09,226
and geographical coordinates to the formats required by Darwin

3647
05:34:09,326 --> 05:34:09,606
Core.

3648
05:34:13,246 --> 05:34:13,706
And then

3649
05:34:14,886 --> 05:34:18,386
taxonomic information can be cleaned in the next step so that

3650
05:34:18,426 --> 05:34:22,046
the core taxon name is

3651
05:34:22,106 --> 05:34:23,426
isolated in one column

3652
05:34:24,386 --> 05:34:28,366
with all the additional information stored in the other appropriate

3653
05:34:28,406 --> 05:34:30,126
Darwin Core fields if possible.

3654
05:34:32,526 --> 05:34:34,386
This clean taxonomic information

3655
05:34:35,746 --> 05:34:39,185
can then be validated through Worms if we're talking about marine

3656
05:34:39,245 --> 05:34:42,986
organisms, or through GBIF taxonomic backbone if we're talking about

3657
05:34:43,046 --> 05:34:46,046
terrestrial organisms. And this process

3658
05:34:47,106 --> 05:34:47,686
allows for the

3659
05:34:48,546 --> 05:34:50,246
correction of small misspellings,

3660
05:34:51,126 --> 05:34:54,926
the resolution of ambiguities, and also the retrieval

3661
05:34:55,006 --> 05:34:58,986
of associated information from the taxonomic record output,

3662
05:34:59,086 --> 05:35:02,646
including the scientific name ID or the taxon rank, for

3663
05:35:02,726 --> 05:35:03,246
instance.

3664
05:35:06,806 --> 05:35:10,006
Then the flat table uploaded can be structured in an

3665
05:35:10,086 --> 05:35:13,466
event core and an occurrence extension tables,

3666
05:35:13,926 --> 05:35:16,946
depending on the fields present in the data set.

3667
05:35:18,486 --> 05:35:21,826
And the app also allows the creation of

3668
05:35:22,566 --> 05:35:26,146
an EMOF table, an extended measurements or facts extension table,

3669
05:35:27,026 --> 05:35:30,886
already in a tidy format. And for that, users need to identify which

3670
05:35:30,946 --> 05:35:34,406
measurements are associated with events and which measurements are

3671
05:35:34,446 --> 05:35:38,186
associated with occurrence. So in this example, for instance,

3672
05:35:39,206 --> 05:35:42,726
temperature would be a measurement associated with a whole event,

3673
05:35:43,246 --> 05:35:47,146
and life stage would be a measurement associated with single occurrences.

3674
05:35:49,206 --> 05:35:53,066
Then measurements or facts can be edited within the app

3675
05:35:53,126 --> 05:35:56,326
in order to be populated with controlled vocabulary.

3676
05:36:00,626 --> 05:36:02,006
Data then goes through

3677
05:36:02,986 --> 05:36:06,786
a quality control step that provides processing logs, summary

3678
05:36:06,846 --> 05:36:07,846
outputs, and

3679
05:36:09,066 --> 05:36:12,426
the flagging of any issues that might have occurred along the standardization

3680
05:36:12,506 --> 05:36:16,226
process. And after going through this quality control

3681
05:36:16,306 --> 05:36:17,246
step, the

3682
05:36:18,106 --> 05:36:20,406
Darwin Core tables are ready to be exported,

3683
05:36:21,846 --> 05:36:25,786
and they then can be used to create a Darwin Core archive

3684
05:36:25,846 --> 05:36:26,246
through

3685
05:36:27,086 --> 05:36:28,506
the gbifscipt.

3686
05:36:31,602 --> 05:36:35,461
The final step. In the final step, users can

3687
05:36:35,542 --> 05:36:39,342
fill in and export a metadata form that was created

3688
05:36:39,842 --> 05:36:42,542
based on the GBIF IPT format

3689
05:36:43,862 --> 05:36:45,922
in order to streamline the creation and

3690
05:36:46,802 --> 05:36:49,842
revision of the metadata associated with

3691
05:36:50,122 --> 05:36:51,482
the resource.

3692
05:36:54,282 --> 05:36:58,062
So the final output of the workflow are

3693
05:36:58,842 --> 05:37:01,622
standard-complying Darwin Core tables and structure metadata,

3694
05:37:02,482 --> 05:37:06,142
allowing for a Darwin Core archive to be built and published in

3695
05:37:06,202 --> 05:37:07,702
accordance with FAIR principles.

3696
05:37:09,002 --> 05:37:12,602
There's some small points that need to be kept in mind, including

3697
05:37:12,662 --> 05:37:14,822
that the Aurora Shiny app does not

3698
05:37:16,102 --> 05:37:18,142
directly create a Darwin Core archive.

3699
05:37:18,662 --> 05:37:22,482
It helps users generate the core data files and

3700
05:37:22,782 --> 05:37:26,582
to compile the metadata, but the Darwin Core archive itself

3701
05:37:26,622 --> 05:37:29,422
needs to be built through the GBIF IPT.

3702
05:37:31,142 --> 05:37:32,722
Also, the tool was

3703
05:37:33,182 --> 05:37:36,602
primarily designed to create

3704
05:37:37,182 --> 05:37:41,022
an event core associated with an occurrence extension and a name of extension,

3705
05:37:41,742 --> 05:37:44,442
or an occurrence core with the name of extension.

3706
05:37:45,522 --> 05:37:49,202
Currently, the app does not support the creation of any other type

3707
05:37:49,322 --> 05:37:51,922
of extensions, which is definitely

3708
05:37:53,022 --> 05:37:56,902
an important improvement to consider for future versions of the

3709
05:37:56,982 --> 05:37:57,142
tool.

3710
05:37:58,782 --> 05:38:02,702
Other improvements also include the adoption of the Catalog of Life

3711
05:38:02,822 --> 05:38:04,162
as GBIF's

3712
05:38:05,422 --> 05:38:09,342
taxonomic reference, and also the inclusion of the option

3713
05:38:09,402 --> 05:38:12,962
to save the process once the standardization has started.

3714
05:38:14,722 --> 05:38:18,662
One final note is that the use of this tool still requires users to

3715
05:38:18,702 --> 05:38:22,602
be familiar with their data and to know their dataset well, and

3716
05:38:22,662 --> 05:38:26,242
also to have a somewhat basic understanding of what the Darwin Core

3717
05:38:26,962 --> 05:38:27,202
is.

3718
05:38:31,262 --> 05:38:33,682
We still believe that this tool represents a valuable

3719
05:38:34,882 --> 05:38:35,042
tool

3720
05:38:36,482 --> 05:38:39,842
to help making standardization of biodiversity data

3721
05:38:40,842 --> 05:38:41,161
more

3722
05:38:42,122 --> 05:38:42,722
widespread.

